{"id": "section-body-01559", "title": "Hypernatremia", "chapterId": "chapter-one", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "The Concept of Emergency Care and Resuscitation", "Electrolyte disturbances", "Hypernatremia"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-01559", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Hypernatremia", "runs": [{"text": "Hypernatremia"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1855"}}, {"id": "body-01560", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Hypernatremia is defined as a plasma sodium concentration of >145mmol/L. Severe hypernatremia is a plasma sodium value of >160 mmol/L. Hypernatremia can be due to loss of water, gain of sodium, or both. Loss of water is the more common denominator. The main cause of hypernatremia is body water loss.", "runs": [{"text": "Hypernatremia is defined as a plasma sodium concentration of >145mmol/L. Severe hypernatremia is a plasma sodium value"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": ">160"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mmol/L."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Hypernatremia"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "can"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "due"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "loss"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "water,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "gain"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "sodium,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "both."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Loss"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "water is the more common denominator. The main cause of hypernatremia is body water loss."}]}, {"id": "body-01561", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Clinical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Presentation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-01562", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Mild and chronic hypernatremia is usually asymptomatic. Severe acute hypernatremia causes CNS symptoms- irritability, lethargy, seizure and coma", "runs": [{"text": "Mild and chronic hypernatremia is usually asymptomatic. Severe acute hypernatremia causes CNS symptoms- irritability, lethargy, seizure and coma"}]}, {"id": "body-01563", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Investigations", "runs": [{"text": "Investigations"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-01565", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Urine specific gravity/osmolality", "runs": [{"text": "Urine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "specific"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "gravity/osmolality"}], "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1855"}}, {"id": "body-01566", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Serum electrolytes", "runs": [{"text": "Serum"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "electrolytes"}], "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1855"}}, {"id": "body-01567", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "\nCreatinine and BUN", "runs": [{"text": "\n"}, {"text": "Creatinine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "BUN"}], "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1855"}}, {"id": "body-01568", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Blood sugar", "runs": [{"text": "Blood"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "sugar"}], "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1855"}}, {"id": "body-01571", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Non-Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Non-Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-01572", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Encourage free water intake", "runs": [{"text": "Encourage"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "free"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "water"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intake"}]}, {"id": "body-01574", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-01575", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "It depends on the severity of hypernatremia and the patient’s volume state.", "runs": [{"text": "It"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "depends"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "on"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "severity"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "hypernatremia"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patient’s"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "volume"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "state."}]}, {"id": "body-01577", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Hypovolemic Hypernatremia", "runs": [{"text": "Hypovolemic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Hypernatremia"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-01578", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "A: sodium chloride 0.9% (IV) until hypovolemia improves.", "runs": [{"text": "A:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "sodium"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "chloride"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "0.9%"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(IV)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "until"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "hypovolemia "}, {"text": "improves."}]}, {"id": "body-01579", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Heading3", "text": "OR", "runs": [{"text": "OR"}], "level": 3, "conjunction": true}, {"id": "body-01580", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "A: sodium chloride 0.45% (IV)", "runs": [{"text": "A:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "sodium"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "chloride"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "0.45%"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(IV)"}]}, {"id": "body-01581", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Heading3", "text": "OR", "runs": [{"text": "OR"}], "level": 3, "conjunction": true}, {"id": "body-01582", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "A: dextrose 5% IV (To replace the water deficit and ongoing losses)", "runs": [{"text": "A:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "dextrose"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "5%"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "IV"}, {"text"